== 3. The Learned and the Wise

#strong[#emph[Secondly,] Satan has his devices to ensnare and destroy the learned and the wise, and that sometimes]

BY working on them to pride themselves#footnote[John 5:44.] in their talents and abilities;#footnote[1 Kgs 22:22, 23, 24, 25. 1 Cor 1:18–29.] and sometimes by drawing them to rest on their talents and abilities; and sometimes by causing them to make light of and slight those who lack their talents#footnote[The truth of this you may see in the learned scribes and Pharisees.] and abilities, though they excel them in grace and holiness; and sometimes by drawing them to engage their talents and abilities in those ways and things that work against the honor of Christ, the joy of the Spirit, the advancement of the gospel and the liberty of the saints, etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that you#super[+] have nothing but what you have received, Christ being the fountain of common gifts as well as of saving#footnote[1 Cor 4:7. #emph[Quicquid es debes creanti, Quicquid potes debes redimenti,] said Bernard: “Whatever you are, you owe to him who made you; and whatever you have, you owe to him who redeemed you.”] grace. “What do you have” (says the apostle) “that you did not receive? And if you did receive it, why do you boast as though you did not?” There are those who would hammer out their own happiness, like the spider climbing up by the thread of her own weaving. Of all the talents and abilities that are in you#super[+], you may well say, as the young man did of his axe, “Oh, my master, it was borrowed!” Oh Lord! all I have is only borrowed from that fountain that fills all the vessels in heaven and on earth, and yet overflows. My gifts are not so much mine#footnote[1 Chr 29:14.] as yours. “From Your own hand we have given to You,” said that princely prophet, etc.

The second remedy against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that men’s leaning and trusting on their own wits, talents and abilities has been their utter overthrow and ruin,#footnote[General councils were seldom successful, because men came with confidence, leaning on their own understanding and seeking victory rather than verity, says one.] as you may see in Ahithophel, in those administrators and princes who engaged against Daniel, and in the scribes and Pharisees. God loves to confute men in their confidences. He who stands on his talents and abilities only stands on quicksand that will certainly fail him. There is nothing in the world that provokes God more to withdraw from the soul than this; and how can the soul stand when his strength has departed from him? Everything a man leans on but God will be a dart that will certainly pierce his heart through and through. Ah! how many in these days have lost their estates, their friends, their lives, their souls, by leaning on their admired talents and abilities! The saints are described#footnote[Song 8:5.] by their leaning on their beloved, the Lord Jesus. He who leans only on the bosom of Christ lives the highest, choicest, safest and sweetest life; miseries always lie at the door of the man who leans on anything below the precious bosom of Christ. Such a man is most in danger, and this is not the least of his plagues, that he thinks himself secure. It is the greatest wisdom in the world to take the wise man’s counsel: “Trust in the #smallcaps[Lord] with all your#footnote[Prov 3:5.] heart, and lean not on your own understanding.”

The third remedy against this device of Satan is to consider that you#super[+] do not surpass others in talents and abilities more than they surpass you in#footnote[Judas and the scribes and Pharisees had #emph[great talents] but no grace; the disciples had grace but weak talents.] grace and holiness. There may be, and often are, great talents and abilities where there is only a little grace, indeed no grace; and there may be, and often is, a great deal of grace where there are only weak talents and abilities. You#super[+] may be higher than others in gifts of knowledge, speech, learning, etc., and those very souls may be higher than you in their communion with God, in their delighting#footnote[Luke 11:1. Mark 8:31, 32, 33. Luke 24:19–28.] in God, in their dependence on God, in their affections toward God, and in their humble, holy and blameless walk before God. Is it not folly and madness in a man to make light of and slight another because he is not as rich in lead or iron as he is, when the other is a thousand thousand times richer than he in silver and gold, in jewels and pearls? And is it not madness and folly in the extreme in those who have greater talents and abilities than others to slight them on that account, when the very persons they make light of and slight have a thousand times more grace than they? And yet, ah! how this evil spirit prevails in the world! It was the sad complaint of Augustine in his time: “The unlearned” (said#footnote[Surgunt indocti & rapiunt coelum and nos cum doctrinis nostris detrudimur in gehennam.] he) “rise up and take heaven by force, and we with all our learning are thrust down to hell.” It is sad to see how many of the rabbis of these times make an idol of their talents and abilities, and with what an eye of pride, scorn and contempt they look on those who lack their talents and who do not worship the idol they have set up in their own hearts. Paul, the great teacher of the Gentiles, wonderfully surpassed the learned teachers and rabbis of our times in all talents and abilities; and yet, ah! how humbly, how tenderly, how sweetly he carries himself toward the lowliest and the#footnote[1 Cor 9:22. 2 Cor 11:29.] weakest: “To the weak I became weak, to win the weak. I have become all things to all men so that by all possible means I might save some.… Who is weak, and I am not weak? Who is led into sin, and I do not burn with grief?… Therefore, if what I eat causes my brother to stumble, I will never eat meat again, so that I will not cause him to stumble.” But ah! how little of this sweet spirit is to be found in the learned teachers of our age, who look sourly and speak bitterly against those who do not see as they see, and cannot speak as they speak. Sirs, the Spirit of#footnote[Isa 60:13, 14, 15, 16, 17.] the Lord (even in despised saints) will be too strong for you#super[+], and his appearances in them in these latter days will be so full of spiritual beauty and glory that they will darken what you are too apt to count and call your glory. The Spirit of the Lord will not allow his choicest jewel, grace, to be always buried under the straw and stubble of talents and gifts.

The fourth remedy against this device of Satan is to consider that there#footnote[Becanus says that the tree of knowledge bears many leaves and little fruit. Ah! would that it were not so with many in these days who once outshone the stars, etc.] is no surer way for men to have their gifts and talents blasted and withered than to pride themselves in them, to rest on them, to make light of and slight those who lack them, and to engage them against those persons, ways and things on which Jesus Christ has set his heart. Ah! how God has blasted and withered the talents and abilities of many among us who were once famous shining lights! How their sun is darkened and their glory clouded! #emph[How]#footnote[Zech 11:17.] #emph[the sword of the Lord is on their arm and on their right eye! How their arm is completely withered and their right eye utterly blinded!] as the prophet speaks. This is matter for humiliation and lamentation. Many precious, discerning saints see this and mourn for it in secret. And oh, that those men were truly aware of God’s withdrawing from them, so that they may repent, keep humble, carry themselves sweetly toward God’s jewels, and lean only on the Lord and not on their talents and understanding; so that the Lord may delight to visit them with his grace at such a rate that their faces may shine more gloriously than ever, and they may be more serviceable to the honor of Christ and the faith of the saints than they have been before, etc.
